.Sh DESCRIPTION
.In atomics.h
provides a single 32-bit mutex primitive, a broadcast signal, a futex wait
and wake pair, a bounded multi-producer multi-consumer ring buffer of
pointers, a spin backoff and a pair of memory fences.
The mutex is built directly on C11 atomics,
.Fn xft_futex_wait
and
.Fn xft_futex_wake ;
it does not depend on
.Xr pthread 3 .
.Pp
The types and state constants are declared in
.In types/atomic_types.h :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef volatile _Atomic(t_u32) t_atomic32;
typedef volatile _Atomic(t_u64) t_atomic64;
typedef volatile _Atomic(t_any) t_atomicptr;
typedef t_atomic32 t_mutex;

typedef struct s_ring_buffer
{
	t_atomicptr	*memory;
	t_size		alloc_bytes;
	t_u32a		size;

	_Alignas(XFT_DESTRUCTIVE_ALIGN)
	t_atomic32	head;
	_Alignas(XFT_DESTRUCTIVE_ALIGN)
	t_atomic32	tail;
}	t_ring_buffer;

typedef enum e_mutex_type : t_u8
{
	xft_lock_fast,
	xft_lock_slow,
	xft_lock_busy,
}	t_mutex_type;

typedef enum e_xft_lock_state : t_u8
{
	xft_unlocked = 0,
	xft_locked = 1,
	xft_contested = 2,
}	t_xft_lock_state;

typedef enum e_xft_atomic_ordering : t_i32
{
	xft_relaxed = __ATOMIC_RELAXED,
	xft_consume = __ATOMIC_CONSUME,
	xft_acquire = __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE,
	xft_release = __ATOMIC_RELEASE,
	xft_acq_rel = __ATOMIC_ACQ_REL,
	xft_seq_cst = __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST,
}	t_xft_atomic_ordering;
.Ed
.Pp
.Vt t_atomic32
is an atomic 32-bit unsigned integer and
.Vt t_mutex
is a
.Vt t_atomic32 ,
accessed through the
.In stdatomic.h
operations.
.Vt t_atomic64
and
.Vt t_atomicptr
are the 64-bit and pointer counterparts of
.Vt t_atomic32 .
Its value is one of
.Dv xft_unlocked ,
.Dv xft_locked
or
.Dv xft_contested :
.Dv xft_unlocked
means the mutex is free,
.Dv xft_locked
means it is held and no other thread has tried to take it, and
.Dv xft_contested
means it is held and another thread has tried to take it and may be asleep
on it in
.Fn xft_futex_wait .
.Pp
.Dv XFT_MUTEX_SPIN
is the number of iterations
.Fn xft_mutex_lock
performs on the
.Dv xft_lock_fast
path before sleeping.
It defaults to 32 and can be redefined before
.In atomics.h
is included.
.Ss Futexes
.Fn xft_futex_wait
sleeps while
.Fa atomic
holds
.Fa value_to_wait .
If
.Fa atomic
holds a different value on entry it returns at once.
On Linux it issues the
.Xr futex 2
system call with
.Dv FUTEX_WAIT ,
without
.Dv FUTEX_PRIVATE_FLAG
or a timeout, and is woken by
.Fn xft_futex_wake
on the same address or by the wake-up the kernel issues for
.Dv CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID .
On Windows it calls
.Fn WaitOnAddress
with an
.Dv INFINITE
timeout.
.Pp
It may return without a matching wake-up, so callers reload
.Fa atomic
and wait again while the value has not changed.
.Pp
.Fn xft_futex_wake
wakes threads sleeping in
.Fn xft_futex_wait
on
.Fa atomic .
On Linux it issues the
.Xr futex 2
system call with
.Dv FUTEX_WAKE ,
without
.Dv FUTEX_PRIVATE_FLAG ,
and wakes at most
.Fa waiters
threads.
The kernel reads
.Fa waiters
as a signed
.Vt int ,
so
.Dv INT32_MAX
wakes every waiter and any value above it wakes one.
On Windows a
.Fa waiters
of 1 calls
.Fn WakeByAddressSingle ,
and any other value calls
.Fn WakeByAddressAll .
Neither function stores to
.Fa atomic ;
the caller updates the value before waking.
.Ss Signals
A signal is a
.Vt t_atomic32
that counts the sends made on it.
A zeroed
.Vt t_atomic32
is a signal that has not been sent.
.Pp
.Fn xft_wait_for_signal
loads the count on entry and blocks until it changes, sleeping in
.Fn xft_futex_wait
and reloading the count with acquire ordering after every wake-up.
It waits for the next send after the call; a send made before the call
does not release it.
.Pp
.Fn xft_send_signal
adds one to the count with release ordering and wakes every waiter through
.Fn xft_futex_wake
with
.Dv INT32_MAX .
Writes made before the call are visible to every thread it releases from
.Fn xft_wait_for_signal .
A signal needs no reset between sends.
.Ss Mutex functions
.Fn xft_mutex_init
stores
.Dv xft_unlocked
into
.Fa mutex .
Nothing is allocated and no system call is made; a mutex in static storage
that has been zeroed is already in the same state.
.Pp
.Fn xft_mutex_lock
acquires
.Fa mutex ,
blocking until it is available.
Every call first attempts a single acquire compare-and-exchange from
.Dv xft_unlocked
to
.Dv xft_locked .
If it succeeds the function returns without touching the slow path.
If it fails, execution continues according to
.Fa type :
.Bl -tag -width "xft_lock_busy"
.It Dv xft_lock_fast
Spin up to
.Dv XFT_MUTEX_SPIN
times.
Each iteration performs a relaxed load; if the mutex reads as
.Dv xft_unlocked
the thread attempts a compare-and-exchange from
.Dv xft_unlocked
to
.Dv xft_contested .
Between attempts the thread calls
.Fn xft_backoff
with the largest power of two not greater than the current iteration
count.
If the spin fails, the thread exchanges the mutex into
.Dv xft_contested
and, on failure, sleeps in
.Fn xft_futex_wait
with the expected value
.Dv xft_contested .
It retries the exchange after each wake-up.
This is the general purpose choice.
.It Dv xft_lock_busy
Spin forever, attempting the same
.Dv xft_contested
exchange and backing off between attempts.
No system call is made.
Suitable when the critical section is short and contention is expected to
be rare.
.It Dv xft_lock_slow
Exchange the mutex into
.Dv xft_contested
and, if the previous value was not
.Dv xft_unlocked ,
sleep in
.Fn xft_futex_wait .
There is no spin phase.
Suitable when the lock is expected to be heavily contended.
.El
.Pp
The
.Fa type
argument selects the failure path only; the initial compare-and-exchange
from
.Dv xft_unlocked
to
.Dv xft_locked
is shared by all three.
Passing a value that is not one of the three enumerators reaches
.Ql __builtin_unreachable .
.Pp
.Fn xft_mutex_unlock
releases
.Fa mutex .
It performs a release exchange of
.Dv xft_unlocked
into the mutex.
If the previous value was
.Dv xft_contested ,
one thread is woken via
.Fn xft_futex_wake .
Otherwise no wake-up is issued, so calling it on a mutex that is already
.Dv xft_unlocked
leaves it unchanged.
.Ss Ring buffers
A
.Vt t_ring_buffer
is a bounded first-in first-out queue of non-null pointers that any number
of threads can submit to and steal from at the same time.
.Fa memory
is an array of
.Fa size
slots, where
.Fa size
is a power of two and a null slot is empty.
.Fa alloc_bytes
is the size of the allocation that holds the array.
.Fa tail
counts the positions handed out to producers and
.Fa head
counts the positions handed out to consumers;
position
.Va p
lives in slot
.Ql p & (size - 1) .
Both counters are 32-bit and wrap around.
.Pp
.Fa head
and
.Fa tail
each start on their own
.Dv XFT_DESTRUCTIVE_ALIGN
boundary, so producers and consumers do not write to the same cache line.
.Dv XFT_DESTRUCTIVE_ALIGN
is 128 bytes on x86_64, aarch64 and powerpc64, 256 on s390x, 32 on 32-bit
arm, mips, hexagon and 32-bit sparc, 16 on m68k and 64 elsewhere.
.Pp
.Fn xft_new_ring_buffer
rounds
.Fa min_size
up to the next power of two, allocates that many slots through
.Fa allocator
with an alignment of
.Dv __GCC_CONSTRUCTIVE_SIZE ,
zeroes the whole allocation and returns the ring buffer with both counters
at 0.
It returns a zeroed
.Vt t_ring_buffer ,
whose
.Fa memory
is a null pointer, when
.Fa min_size
is 0 or above 2^31, when the slot array does not fit in a
.Vt t_size ,
or when the allocation fails.
On 32-bit targets the
.Vt t_size
limit rejects every
.Fa min_size
above 2^29.
.Pp
.Fn xft_destroy_ring_buffer
returns the slot array of
.Fa rb
to
.Fa allocator .
It does not modify
.Fa rb .
.Pp
.Fn xft_ring_buffer_submit
appends
.Fa ptr .
It returns
.Dv KO
when the buffer holds
.Fa size
pointers.
Otherwise it claims the position at
.Fa tail
with a weak release compare-and-exchange, stores
.Fa ptr
into its slot with release ordering and returns
.Dv OK .
.Pp
.Fn xft_ring_buffer_steal
removes the oldest pointer.
It returns a null pointer when
.Fa head
equals
.Fa tail .
Otherwise it claims the position at
.Fa head
with a weak release compare-and-exchange, stores a null pointer into its
slot with release ordering and returns the pointer the slot held.
.Pp
Both functions load
.Fa head ,
.Fa tail
and the slot with acquire ordering and retry at once when another thread
wins the compare-and-exchange.
A slot can be in flight: claimed by a producer that has not stored its
pointer, which
.Fn xft_ring_buffer_steal
reads as null, or claimed by a consumer that has not cleared it, which
.Fn xft_ring_buffer_submit
reads as the previous pointer.
On an in-flight slot both functions call
.Fn xft_backoff
and retry.
The count starts at 1 and grows by one with each in-flight slot the call
meets, with no upper bound; a failed compare-and-exchange does not reset it.
The window between the claim and the store is a few instructions, so the
wait is short unless the thread that claimed the slot is preempted or
stopped.
.Pp
Pointers come out in the order their positions were claimed.
Writes made before
.Fn xft_ring_buffer_submit
are visible to the thread whose
.Fn xft_ring_buffer_steal
returns that pointer.
.Ss Memory fences
.Fn xft_thread_fence
issues a hardware memory fence with the memory order given by
.Fa memorder .
.Fn xft_signal_fence
issues a compiler fence with the same argument.
.Pp
Each
.Vt t_xft_atomic_ordering
constant has the value of the matching
.Dv __ATOMIC_*
macro, so it can be passed as the order argument of the
.In stdatomic.h
explicit operations and of the
.Fn __atomic_*
builtins.
The mutex functions use these constants for every atomic access.
.Ss Backoff
.Fn xft_backoff
executes
.Fa n
relaxation instructions:
.Ql pause
on x86_64,
.Ql yield
on aarch64, or an empty memory-clobbering
.Ql asm
statement elsewhere.
It does not sleep or make a system call, and an
.Fa n
of 0 returns at once.
.Pp
The mutex spin loops call it with a count derived from the current
iteration number through
.Fn xft_last_pow2 ,
so the number of instructions in a single backoff step stays bounded by
the loop's own iteration cap.
The ring buffer functions call it with a per-call count that starts at 1
and grows by one on every retry on an in-flight slot, so the pause grows
linearly for as long as the slot stays in flight.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_new_ring_buffer
returns the new ring buffer, or a zeroed
.Vt t_ring_buffer
on failure.
.Pp
.Fn xft_ring_buffer_submit
returns
.Dv OK
when
.Fa ptr
has been stored and
.Dv KO
when the buffer is full.
.Pp
.Fn xft_ring_buffer_steal
returns the oldest pointer, or a null pointer when the buffer is empty.
.Pp
The other functions return nothing.
.Fn xft_mutex_lock
blocks until it holds the mutex; it has no failure mode.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

static t_mutex	g_lock;

void	init(void)
{
	xft_mutex_init(&g_lock);
}

void	critical_section(void)
{
	xft_mutex_lock(&g_lock, xft_lock_fast);
	/* ... */
	xft_mutex_unlock(&g_lock);
}

void	queue(t_any job)
{
	const t_allocator	alloc = xft_page_allocator();
	t_ring_buffer		rb;
	t_any				out;

	rb = xft_new_ring_buffer(alloc, 64);
	if (rb.memory == nullptr)
		return ;
	while (xft_ring_buffer_submit(&rb, job) == KO)
		xft_backoff(1);
	out = xft_ring_buffer_steal(&rb);
	/* ... */
	xft_destroy_ring_buffer(alloc, &rb);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_alloc 3 ,
.Xr xft_os 3 ,
.Xr xft_threads 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
.Fn xft_mutex_lock
with a
.Fa type
outside
.Dv xft_lock_fast ,
.Dv xft_lock_slow
and
.Dv xft_lock_busy
reaches
.Ql __builtin_unreachable
and compiles to undefined behaviour at the call site.
.Pp
.Fn xft_wait_for_signal
misses a send made between the caller's last check of the shared state
and the call, and sleeps until the following send.
Use it only when the sender sends after the waiters are already waiting,
or when the sender keeps sending until the waiters respond.
.Pp
A waiter that sleeps through exactly 2^32 sends reads the count it loaded
on entry and keeps sleeping.
.Pp
The mutex does not track its owner and is not recursive; locking it twice
from the same thread deadlocks.
.Pp
.Dv xft_lock_busy
mutexes can starve other threads on a uniprocessor or an over-subscribed
system, since the spin loop never yields through the kernel.
.Pp
A null pointer marks an empty slot, so it cannot be stored.
Passing a null
.Fa ptr
to
.Fn xft_ring_buffer_submit ,
or a ring buffer whose
.Fa memory
is a null pointer to
.Fn xft_ring_buffer_submit
or
.Fn xft_ring_buffer_steal ,
reaches
.Ql __builtin_unreachable .
So does an
.Fa allocator
with a null vtable, or a null
.Fa allocate
or
.Fa free
entry, passed to
.Fn xft_new_ring_buffer
or
.Fn xft_destroy_ring_buffer .
.Pp
Calling
.Fn xft_ring_buffer_submit
or
.Fn xft_ring_buffer_steal
from a signal handler on a ring buffer that the interrupted thread is
inside
.Fn xft_ring_buffer_submit
or
.Fn xft_ring_buffer_steal
on can spin forever: the handler waits on a slot that the interrupted
thread cannot finish until the handler returns.
Signals whose handlers do not touch the ring buffer only delay the
interrupted call.
.Pp
A thread stopped between claiming a slot and storing to it, by preemption
or a debugger, makes every thread that reaches that slot spin in
.Fn xft_backoff
until it resumes.
The spin never yields through the kernel, so on a uniprocessor or an
over-subscribed system the spinning threads can hold the CPU the stopped
thread needs.
.Pp
.Fn xft_destroy_ring_buffer
must not run while another thread uses
.Fa rb ,
and
.Fa allocator
must be the allocator that created it.
